0
00:00:00,830 --> 00:00:08,390
Hello everyone, the title of my presentation is "Generating the Cloud Motion Winds Field from Satellite Cloud Imagery Using Deep Learning Approach".

1
00:00:09,430 --> 00:00:12,950
My name is Chao Tan, from Chongqing University of Technology.

2
00:00:14,340 --> 00:00:19,860
Cloud motion winds field is derived from geostationary satellite-observed motions of clouds features.

3
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:30,260
The algorithm estimates the average motion vectors of the atmosphere in a certain area based on the movement of the same cloud mass in two consecutive infrared satellite cloud imageries.

4
00:00:31,460 --> 00:00:38,700
Cloud motion winds field have been shown to provide reasonable estimates of the ambient tropospheric wind, and its application is wide ranging.

5
00:00:39,610 --> 00:00:44,770
It contributes to forecast diagnostics of weather conditions, as well as numerical weather prediction.

6
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:52,980
In addition, cloud motion winds field can also provide basic guidance for such field as ultraviolet index and solar energy prediction.

7
00:00:54,230 --> 00:00:58,630
With the application of image processing and machine learning technology in various fields,

8
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:05,200
we noticed that the cloud motion wind algorithm is similar to the task of optical flow estimation in computer vision.

9
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:12,940
Therefore, we formalized the cloud motion wind estimation task as the problem of unsupervised dense optical flow prediction.

10
00:01:14,030 --> 00:01:19,830
Unlike traditional image processing methods, we use deep learning methods to learn the feature representation automatically,

11
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:24,340
and it also greatly improve the calculation speed and efficiency of the algorithm.

12
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:28,680
It is worth noting that duo to the uncertainty of atmospheric movement,

13
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:33,620
the brightness consistency between two adjacent satellite cloud imageries is not guaranteed,

14
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:39,780
so the unsupervised optic flow estimation based on photometric consistency is not suitable for use.

15
00:01:40,670 --> 00:01:46,830
In addition, cloud motion winds estimation often requires more robust performance than optical flow prediction,

16
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:51,240
and a large-scale dataset is also required for training the deep learning models.

17
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:59,590
In order to train our deep learning model, we first propose a brand-new large-scale cloud motion winds dataset named CMWD,

18
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:05,140
which contains more than 6300 training samples and 700 testing samples.

19
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:13,490
Each sample consists of two geostationary satellite cloud imageries, taken a one-hour intervals over a vast of the Pacific Northwest.

20
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:22,090
In order to visualize the output optical flow, we encode the generated two-dimensional flow field with color in the HSV color model.

21
00:02:22,950 --> 00:02:27,750
During the training phase, we use PCA-Flow to generate it as the supervision for the model.

22
00:02:28,490 --> 00:02:35,770
In addition, we also convert the generated vector field into two visualization products, one is the common dense vector arrow diagram.

23
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:42,910
The other is the flux streamline diagram, which can continuously display the direction and intensity of the wind field.

24
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:50,630
We use FlowNet-S as a reference network and this architecture consists of a contractive part followed by an expanding part.

25
00:02:51,660 --> 00:02:59,700
The contractive part takes as input two infrared cloud imageries stacked together, and processes them with a cascade of stride deconvolution layers.

26
00:03:00,690 --> 00:03:03,690
The expanding part implements a "skip layer" architecture

27
00:03:04,030 --> 00:03:12,190
that combines information from various levels of contractive part with "up convolving" layers to iteratively refine the coarse cloud motion winds filed predictions.

28
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:21,200
In addition, we use endpoint error (EPE) during the training process, and we use several intermediate losses placed at various stages of the expansionary part.

29
00:03:22,230 --> 00:03:26,910
The intermediate losses are meant to guide earlier layers more directly towards the final objective.

30
00:03:27,990 --> 00:03:34,030
We first use the paired infrared satellite cloud imagery on CMWD dataset to conduct the experiment.

31
00:03:34,860 --> 00:03:40,740
It can be seen from the experimental results that our model can well estimate the upper-air wind motion at its location,

32
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:46,220
whether it is a large air mass movement or a small-to-medium-scale anticyclonic spiral movement.

33
00:03:47,410 --> 00:03:54,690
We also try to use single infrared cloud imagery to estimate the cloud motion winds field, which is not feasible for conventional methods.

34
00:03:55,790 --> 00:03:59,270
However, because deep neural network can learn from a large amount of data, 

35
00:03:59,720 --> 00:04:06,080
many basic patterns of atmospheric motion can be detected by the model, and then predict through the state of the whole cloud system.

36
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:10,920
From the experimental results, it can be seen that even with only one input image,

37
00:04:11,430 --> 00:04:16,510
our model is still able to make a rough estimate of cloud motion winds field under the current situation.

38
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:23,000
Meanwhile, our model can separate different cloud structures in cloud imagery and make different estimates accordingly.

39
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:30,280
Here are the dynamic image samples with paired and single cloud imagery superimposed on flux streamline diagram,

40
00:04:30,890 --> 00:04:34,170
from which we can intuitively see the effectiveness of our proposed method.

41
00:04:35,250 --> 00:04:40,970
In this work, we propose to use a deep convolutional neural network to estimate the cloud motion winds field.

42
00:04:42,180 --> 00:04:46,900
Meanwhile, we also present a large-scale cloud motion winds dataset for deep learning research.

43
00:04:48,110 --> 00:04:55,670
The experimental results illustrate the efficient performance of our model, and it can also handle the case without paired satellite cloud imagery input.

44
00:04:57,100 --> 00:04:58,100
That's all. Thank you.
